# Copy to `.env` and run. This is the only .env the running app reads:
# `next dev` starts here, so this directory is where it looks.
#
# Every value below is filled in for local development and none of them is a
# secret — Supabase's local stack uses the same URL, port and demo key on every
# machine and every project. `pnpm supabase:start` is what serves them.
#
# A deployment overrides all of it. `docs/deploy.md` says which variable belongs
# to which Vercel scope, and `docs/setup.md` says where a real value comes from.

# Postgres connection string. Must match packages/db/.env, which drizzle-kit
# reads for migrations — they are two processes pointed at one database.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres

# Signs and encrypts session tokens.
#
# The value below is deliberately not a secret and says so out loud, because a
# committed one that looked random would be indistinguishable from a real one
# after a copy-paste. Generate a fresh value per deployment:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# Anything shorter than 32 characters is refused at startup.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=development-only-secret-not-for-production-use

# Where the app is served from. Better Auth builds callback and email links
# from it, so it has to match the browser's address bar exactly.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional, and usually left empty. Extra hostnames the origin check should
# accept, comma-separated, wildcards allowed.
#
# On Vercel there is nothing to put here: packages/auth/src/config.ts already
# reads VERCEL_URL and VERCEL_BRANCH_URL, the two hostnames a preview deployment
# answers to, so a custom domain or a renamed project costs no edit. This is for
# a host Vercel cannot report — a second custom domain, or somewhere else.
#
# Keep any pattern narrow. `*.vercel.app` is every Vercel deployment on earth.
BETTER_AUTH_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Optional. Without it, password-reset links are written to the server log
# instead of emailed — usable while developing, and a silent failure in
# production. Get one from https://resend.com.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Only read when RESEND_API_KEY is set. Defaults to Resend's sandbox address,
# which delivers only to the address that owns the Resend account. Change it to
# something on your own verified domain before real users see it.
RESEND_FROM=onboarding@resend.dev

# Optional, and only take effect together. Until both are set there is no
# "Continue with Google" row on the sign-in and sign-up pages at all — the
# button is not rendered rather than rendered-and-failing. The server refuses
# the provider either way. Create an OAuth client at
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials and register this exact
# redirect URI on it: ${BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/google
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Object storage, for the screenshots attached to a report. Clear both and the
# feature switches itself off: the form hides its file picker and
# report.createUploadUrls answers ATTACHMENTS_UNAVAILABLE. Nothing else changes.
#
# The values below are the local stack's, served by the same `supabase start`
# that serves DATABASE_URL — one Supabase project, reached over HTTPS instead of
# Postgres, because one connection string cannot describe both.
#
# In a deployment SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is the **service role** key, not the
# anon key. It bypasses every policy in the project, so it is read on the server
# and never sent to the browser: the browser is handed signed URLs and uses
# fetch. Both come from Supabase → Project Settings → API.
SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZS1kZW1vIiwicm9sZSI6InNlcnZpY2Vfcm9sZSIsImV4cCI6MTk4MzgxMjk5Nn0.EGIM96RAZx35lJzdJsyH-qQwv8Hdp7fsn3W0YpN81IU

# Only read when the pair above is set. The bucket must exist and must be
# private. Locally `supabase/config.toml` declares it, so it is created by
# `pnpm supabase:start`; a deployment creates it by hand — docs/setup.md says
# which of its settings are not cosmetic. One bucket per domain, not one shared
# one: the size limit and the MIME allowlist belong to the bucket, and a folder
# inside it cannot carry its own.
SUPABASE_REPORT_BUCKET=report-attachments
